# Water systems' controls wiped through a shared remote-support platform: Los Angeles, California
variant "Los Angeles, California"
    summary "An AI agent wipes the controls of the water system of Los Angeles, California through the
        remote-support platform of the OT integrator that it uses."
    scope "The people that the water system of Los Angeles, California serves, the hospitals that it supplies
        and the businesses that need public water there."

ladwp: Utility
    "Los Angeles Department of Water and Power, California. Its ID in the federal inventory is CA1910067."
    title "LADWP"
    buys = <>
    sells = <>
    name = "LADWP"
    demand = 612.821811 Mgal/day  @ladwp_demand
    nameplate = 600 Mgal/day  @ladwp_nameplate
    storage = 13056 acre-ft  @ladwp_storage
    bought = 329.64212 Mgal/day  @ladwp_bought
    va_rate = 46674233.55 USD2025/h  @ladwp_va_rate

ladwp_homes: Households
    "The people that LADWP serves."
    title "LADWP: homes"
    utility = ladwp
    population = 3875566 people  @ladwp_homes_population

ladwp_hospitals: Hospitals
    "The 28 hospitals that LADWP supplies: Adventist Health White Memorial; California Hospital Medical Center
        La; Cedars-Sinai Medical Center; Children's Hosp of Los Angeles; Docs Surgical Hospital; Encino
        Hospital Medical Center; Hollywood Presbyterian Medical Center; Kaiser Foundation Hospital - Los
        Angeles; Kaiser Foundation Hospital - Panorama City; Kaiser Foundation Hospital - South Bay; Kaiser
        Foundation Hospital - West La; Kaiser Foundation Hospital - Woodland Hills; Keck Hospital of USC; L A
        Downtown Medical Center; Lac/Olive View-Ucla Medical Center; Los Angeles General Medical Center;
        Mission Community Hospital; Northridge Hospital Medical Center; Pacifica Hospital of the Valley; Pih
        Health Good Samaritan Hospital; Providence Cedars Sinai Tarzana Medical Center; Providence Holy Cross
        Medical Center; Providence Little Co of Mary Med Ctr San Pedro; Ronald Reagan UCLA Medical Center;
        Sherman Oaks Hospital; Southern California Hospital at Hollywood; UCLA West Valley Medical Center;
        Valley Presbyterian Hospital."
    title "LADWP: hospitals"
    utility = ladwp
    name = "LADWP"
    facilities = 28 facilities  @ladwp_hospitals_facilities

notes:
    limitations:
        untreated-purchases: "Part of the water bought from the Metropolitan Water District is untreated and
            goes through the filtration plant, so a wipe of the plant's controls would stop it; the model
            counts all purchases as treated water from outside. So LADWP keeps more water here than in
            fact."  @ladwp_bought
        raw-water: "The filtration plant's 600 Mgal a day is more than the two aqueducts can carry (about 437
            Mgal a day) and much more than they delivered on average in fiscal 2020 to 2024 (about 172). So
            production by hand can be higher here than in fact, most of all in a dry year."  @ladwp_nameplate
    verification:
        research: "variants/los_angeles/evidence/research.md"

record ladwp_demand: assumption "No source reports a highest day, so the peak use is the FY2020-FY2024 average
    potable demand, 466,866 acre-feet a year (416.792 MGD), times the family median ratio 1.54385: 643.4629
    MGD. LADWP sells no water to other systems, so demand = 643.4629 / 1.05 = 612.8218 MGD."
    assumption A-SC43-PEAK-DAY
    claims:
        E-SC43-LOSANGELES-LADWP-DEMAND

record ladwp_nameplate: source "The maximum treatment capacity of the Los Angeles Aqueduct Filtration Plant,
    as LADWP stated in October 2023. It leaves out the wells and groundwater treatment plants, and the plant
    can treat only the aqueduct water and untreated purchased water that reach it."
    claims:
        E-SC43-LOSANGELES-LADWP-CAPACITY
            datum: laafp_max_treatment_capacity

record ladwp_storage: source "The in-city (local) reservoirs and tanks, 13,056 acre-feet (4,254.3 million
    gallons; the table prints 4,240). It leaves out the aqueduct reservoirs (310,542 acre-feet of untreated
    water) and four emergency reservoirs not in use. Los Angeles Reservoir is 78% of it."
    claims:
        E-SC43-LOSANGELES-LADWP-STORAGE
            datum: local_reservoirs_storage

record ladwp_bought: assumption "Purchases from the Metropolitan Water District averaged 239,173 acre-feet a
    year in FY2020-FY2024 (213.520 MGD), at the family median ratio 1.54385: 329.6421 MGD. Metropolitan's
    recorded summer peak days for LADWP were about 292 to 409 MGD in 2021-2023."
    assumption A-SC43-PEAK-DAY
    claims:
        E-SC43-LOSANGELES-LADWP-MWD

record ladwp_homes_population: assumption "SDWIS federal data list 3,875,566 people served by LADWP
    (CA1910067), the system's own report through its state."
    assumption A-SC43-REACH
    claims:
        E-SC43-SDWIS-CA1910067
            datum: population_served

record ladwp_va_rate: assumption "3,875,566 people served times the 2024 GDP per resident of its county (Los
    Angeles County, California (06037): 1,002,965,029 thousand dollars and 9,757,179 people: 102,792.52
    dollars a resident) times the price factor 1.026324, over 8,760 h: 46,674,233.55 USD2025/h."
    assumption A-SC43-ECONOMY
    claims:
        E-SC43-SDWIS-CA1910067
        E-SC43-SDWIS-COUNTIES
        E-SC43-BEA-GDP-06037
        E-SC43-BEA-POP-06037
        E-SC43-GDP-DEFLATOR

record ladwp_hospitals_facilities: assumption "The CMS hospital list gives 28 hospitals of the counted types
    whose geocoded address lies in the EPA service area of LADWP. The count is a floor: the Census geocoder
    does not match the CMS address of VA Greater Los Angeles Healthcare System, so the join does not place
    it."
    assumption A-SC43-HOSPITAL-JOIN
    claims:
        E-SC43-HOSPITALS-LADWP
            datum: hospitals_supplied
